 ## Background
-The Fast Ray-Surface Intersection method was introduced as a faster alternative to exact ray-surface intersection techniques in the context of cell-based first-hit ray casting [S1, unofficial]. Exact methods, such as those by Lin et al. and Parker et al., invert trilinear interpolation to find all points assigned a specific density value, which is computationally expensive [S1, unofficial]. The fast intersection method trades this accuracy for speed by using linear interpolation on subdivided paths [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader family of approximate ray-surface intersection techniques used in volume rendering to optimize performance [S1, S2, unofficial].
